speechtext:
The hon. member says "and it will do so". Time will tell. As I was saying, if the present government was to remain in power for many more years I can well understand how many of the provinces might feel that they are protected by a fixed equalization payment based on the present scale of payments for this year. We all know that during the 1950's, the annual output of the Canadian economy more than doubled in value, rising from $16 billion to $35 billion. The gross national product rose quite steeply in the years 1944-56, despite the short setback 90205-6-99}
Dominion-Provincial Relations in the recession period of 1953-54. It is also a fact that real consumption per capita declined in 1957 and in 1958 and by 1959 it was only fractionally higher than it was in 1956; and this decline happened under a Conservative administration, that of the present government.
For the first nine months of 1960 total output was only 2.9 per cent higher than it was in the corresponding period in 1959, but almost half of this increase can be attributed to higher prices of consumer goods. Those figures cannot be denied because they have just been released in the report of the Canadian Bank of Commerce. Under the continuation of such conditions the suggestion of the Prime Minister might be considered as acceptable to certain provinces. But every right thinking Canadian knows that this government has lost the confidence of the people.
